  "homeActivities": [
    {
      "text": "Activity 1 — What is really inside soil?\nPut a spoonful of soil from the garden or a plant pot onto a white plate. Look at it closely with a magnifying glass (the zoom on a phone\ncamera works too). Ask him to name everything he can see: little stones, sand, bits of dead leaf, maybe a tiny insect or a worm. Soil is a\nmixture, not one single thing. Wash hands afterwards.\nActivity 2 — Be a geologist\nA geologist is a scientist who studies rocks. Collect five stones outside and sort them: big and small, rough and smooth, light and dark,\nheavy and light. Ask him to invent his own way of grouping them — then explain his rule to you.\nActivity 3 — Talk about litter\nAsk him: \"What happens if a bird tries to eat a plastic bag? What if a turtle swallows a bottle?\" Six‑year‑olds care deeply about this. Then\npick up three pieces of litter together on your next walk.\npond stream river lake ocean\nfrogs small, moving the Nile big and still the biggest, and salty\nThe water family, from the smallest to the biggest.\n",
      "safety": "An adult stays with the child for every activity. No glass, no sockets, no flames, and nothing small enough to swallow is left with a child\nalone. Water activities are done in a bowl or the sink, never near electrical items.",
